```rust
use std::time::{Duration, SystemTime};

// Wall clock decides whether a lease is still valid and when a loop times out
fn run_while_leased(lease_expiry: SystemTime) {
    let start = SystemTime::now();
    loop {
        // BUG: NTP can step the clock backward or forward; a paused thread may
        // pass this check after the lease has already expired.
        if SystemTime::now() < lease_expiry {
            process_write_request();
        }
        // BUG: elapsed() on SystemTime can fail, yield zero, or jump by hours.
        if start.elapsed().unwrap_or_default() > Duration::from_secs(10) {
            break;
        }
    }
}
```

counterexample:
```rust
use std::time::Instant;

fn run_with_timeout() {
    let start = Instant::now();
    loop {
        do_work();
        if start.elapsed() > std::time::Duration::from_secs(10) {
            break;
        }
    }
}
```

`Instant`-based measurement of local elapsed intervals does not depend on wall-clock adjustments. Wall-clock time remains appropriate for real-world timestamps and intentional calendar-time comparisons, just not as sole correctness evidence for the cases above. A local monotonic check before a lease-protected write is not by itself a complete distributed lease-safety counterexample.
